WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
Zero Trust responds to changes in how organizations use technology.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.280
Employees work from offices, homes, customer sites, and mobile locations.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:12.105
Applications may run in data centers, public cloud platforms, software services,

00:00:12.105 --> 00:00:13.000
and partner environments.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.945
Data also moves across organizational boundaries.

00:00:15.945 --> 00:00:19.970
Teams collaborate with suppliers, contractors, customers, and service providers.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:23.730
A simple distinction between inside and outside no longer describes where important work

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:25.280
occurs or where risk exists.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.420
Traditional perimeter controls remain valuable,

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:31.410
but network location alone provides limited evidence about whether an access request

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:32.000
should be allowed.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.040
A request from an internal address can still involve an unmanaged device,

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:39.000
excessive privilege, or an identity showing unexpected behavior.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.945
Cloud and software-as-a-service adoption also change enforcement points.

00:00:41.945 --> 00:00:46.035
Users may connect directly to applications without passing through a traditional

00:00:46.035 --> 00:00:47.000
enterprise network,

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.970
so identity, application policy, and service telemetry become increasingly important.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:55.250
Remote work and mobile devices require security decisions across changing networks.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:01:00.120
Organizations need a consistent way to evaluate access whether the request originates in a

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.000
headquarters office,

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.000
a home network, or a managed mobile connection.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.000
Zero Trust supports risk management by reducing reliance on broad, persistent access.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.000
It encourages organizations to grant access to specific resources based on business need

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.000
and to reassess that access as relevant conditions change.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.945
The strategy can also limit the effect of a security failure.

00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:21.945
If one identity, device, or segment is affected,

00:01:21.945 --> 00:01:25.625
narrowly scoped permissions and stronger policy boundaries can reduce unnecessary access

00:01:25.625 --> 00:01:27.280
to unrelated resources.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:31.245
Organizations adopt Zero Trust gradually to improve verification, visibility,

00:01:31.245 --> 00:01:34.000
and control across this distributed environment.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.530
It complements other security practices rather than replacing identity management,

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.000
monitoring, network security, or incident response.
